1.2.1.6 Arrowheads and Filled Areas
...................................

‘lines’, ‘explicit’ and ‘parametric’ accept ‘head_end=true’,
‘head_start=true’ and ‘head_count=n’ (n heads evenly spaced along the
curve by length).  Heads follow the curve's direction on screen, so they
stay correct under ‘aspect_ratio’; ‘head_size’ scales them.

By default each head follows the curve's local direction
(‘head_direction="tangent"’), as in most plotting libraries.  For wavy
or noisy curves, ‘head_direction="chord"’ points each head along the
curve's overall direction instead: from the point one head-spacing of
length back (the start of the curve, for a single ‘head_end’).

A head's tip sits on its data point and the head extends back over the
curve (Plotly can only offset markers backwards).  For a wavy line such
as a photon, use a decoration (below): with ‘head_end=true’ it leaves a
straight run at the end, so the head points along the line and the
wiggle stops near where the head begins.


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: Decorations,  Next: Diagrams,  Prev: Arrowheads and Filled Areas,  Up: Examples

1.2.1.7 Decorations
...................

‘decoration="snake"’ (a sine wave) or ‘decoration="zigzag"’ on ‘lines’,
‘explicit’ or ‘parametric’ draws a wave that follows the curve, like
TikZ's decorations, so a wavy photon or a zigzag singularity is just a
straight line with a decoration:

ax_draw2d(
  lines([[0, 0], [1, 1]], decoration="snake", decoration_wavelength=0.12,
        decoration_amplitude=0.03, head_end=true, head_size=1.2),
  lines([[-1, 1.2], [1, 1.2]], decoration="zigzag", decoration_wavelength=0.09,
        decoration_amplitude=0.025, color="red"),
  aspect_ratio=true
)$

   • ‘decoration_wavelength’ (default: the curve's length / 10) and
     ‘decoration_amplitude’ (default: wavelength / 4) are in data units,
     and the wave is offset along the curve's normal in data space, so
     it looks right with ‘aspect_ratio=true’.  The wavelength is
     adjusted slightly so that a whole number of waves fits and the wave
     ends back on the curve.
   • ‘decoration_pre’ and ‘decoration_post’ leave the first and last
     stretch of the curve straight.  They default to 0, except that
     ‘head_start=true’ / ‘head_end=true’ make them half a wavelength, so
     end heads point along the curve rather than along the last wiggle.
   • Heads placed along the curve with ‘head_count’ follow the wave; add
     ‘head_direction="chord"’ to point them along the curve instead.
   • A line with gaps is decorated piece by piece.

The algorithm is specified independently of ax-plots, with a
plain-Maxima reference implementation that draws with ‘plot2d’/gnuplot,
in this gist
(https://gist.github.com/cmsd2/b19d20316b62719e10cd9e3840cdf2d2).

‘explicit’ accepts the draw package's ‘filled_func’: ‘filled_func=true’
fills between the curve and y = 0, and ‘filled_func=g(x)’ fills between
the curve and g, using ‘fill_color’.

ax_draw2d(
  explicit(sin(x), x, 0, %pi, filled_func=sin(x)/2, fill_color="rgba(0,0,255,0.2)"),
  parametric(cos(t), sin(t), t, 0, 3*%pi/2, head_end=true, head_count=2),
  aspect_ratio=true
)$

Like the draw package, ‘filled_func’ is sticky when given at the top
level: it applies to every later ‘explicit’ until reset with
‘filled_func=false’.


