1.4.2 Reactive plots
--------------------

With the reactive (https://github.com/cmsd2/maxima-reactive) package
(‘mxpm install reactive’), a plot can depend on named parameters, called
_signals_, that the reader changes with a slider or other control.  The
host shows a control for every signal in the plot and redraws the plot
when one changes.

A signal is created by a widget function and stays symbolic, so it can
be used anywhere in an expression, including inside ‘diff’ or
‘integrate’:

Function                    Control                   Value
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘widget_slider(name, lo,    slider                    a number in ‘[lo,
hi)’                                                  hi]’, initially
                                                      ‘lo’
‘widget_number(name, lo,    number box                a number in ‘[lo,
hi, initial)’                                         hi]’
‘widget_checkbox(name,      checkbox                  ‘0.0’ or ‘1.0’
initial)’
‘widget_dropdown(name,      drop-down list            one of the
[c1, c2, ...])’                                       choices

‘signal(name)’ refers to an existing signal by name, and
‘reactive_reset()’ clears them all.  See the ‘reactive’ documentation
for the rest of its API.

* Menu:

* Examples::
* How it works::

1.4.2.1 Examples
................

load("ax-plots")$
load("reactive")$

/* One slider */
ax_draw2d(explicit(sin(widget_slider("freq", 0.1, 5) * x), x, -%pi, %pi),
          yrange=[-1.2, 1.2])$

/* A signal bound to a variable can be used more than once */
d : widget_slider("damping", 0.05, 1.5)$
ax_draw2d(explicit(exp(-d*x) * cos(6*x), x, 0, 6),
          explicit(exp(-d*x), x, 0, 6, dash="dot"))$

/* 3D works the same way */
ax_draw3d(explicit(sin(widget_slider("kx", 0.2, 3) * x) * cos(y),
                   x, -%pi, %pi, y, -%pi, %pi))$

The ‘examples/reactive-sliders.macnb’ notebook has more.


File: ax-plots.info,  Node: How it works,  Prev: ,  Up: Reactive plots

1.4.2.2 How it works
....................

A plot whose objects contain signals carries a ‘_reactive’ block listing
them (name, kind, range, current value), and is registered under a view
id.  When a control changes, the host sets the signal and asks ax-plots
to draw the view again with the new value.  Plots without signals are
unaffected, and nothing changes when ‘reactive’ isn't loaded.

This needs ax-plots 0.2.0 or later with ‘reactive’ at commit ‘897a88b’
or later, and a host that renders the controls: Aximar built from its
June 2026 ‘master’ onwards.  ‘animate’ and signals can't be combined in
one plot.

See also: ‘ax_draw2d’, ‘ax_draw3d’, ‘animate’


